<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<worksheet>
  <title>2.5. The wave equation Intro</title>
  <p><alert>MTH 401</alert></p>



  <definition xml:id="def-wave-eqn-1d">
    <statement>
      <p>
        The <term>wave equation</term> representing the height of the string <m>u(x,t)</m> 
        in one dimension
        with wave speed <m>\ds v=\sqrt{\frac{T_0}{\rho}}</m> (representing the propagation speed of the wave), 
        constant horizontal tension <m>T_0</m> (i.e. the string is <em>perfectly elastic</em>),
        and density <m>\rho</m>, is:
        <md>
          \ds \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} = v^2 \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}.
        </md>
        In three dimensions the wave equation becomes 
        <md>
          \ds \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} = v^2 \nabla^2 u
        </md>
        where <m>\ds \nabla^2 u= \frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial x^2}+\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial y^2}+\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial z^2}</m> is the Laplacian of <m>u</m>.  
      </p>

    </statement>
  </definition>

  <exercise workspace="2in">
    <statement>
      <p>Let's consider the wave equation in a string of length <m>L</m>
  where both ends are clamped:
  <md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial t^2} \amp  =  v^2\frac{\partial^2 u}{\partial
      x^2}</mrow>
<mrow>u(0,t) \amp  =  0</mrow>
<mrow>u(L,t) \amp  =  0</mrow>
</md>
  Find the general solution to this equation. (We use the steps steps as if to solve the heat equation. What technique do you think you can apply here? What technique do we usually turn to?)</p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-5-soln">
      <p>
        Separation of variables: <m>u(x,t) = X(x)T(t)</m>, plug in and rearrange to get 
        <md>
          \frac{1}{v^2} \frac{T''}{T} = \frac{X''}{X} = -k
        </md>
        which separates into <m>T''=-kv^2T</m> and 
        <m>X''=-kX</m> with <m>{X(0)=0, X(L)=0}</m> (the Sturm-Liouville problem). 
        This has solutions <m>{k=(\frac{n\pi}{L})^2}</m> for <m>{n=1,2,\ldots}</m> with
        <m>{X_n = \sin(\frac{n\pi x}{L})}</m>.  Plugging back into <m>{T'' = -(\frac{n\pi v}{L})^2T}</m> we have
        solutions 
        <md>
          T_n = A_n \cos(\frac{n\pi v t}{L})+B_n \sin(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}).
        </md>
         Thus by superposition the general solution is:
         <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \cos\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) +  B_n \sin\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)
         </md>
         
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise>
    <introduction>
      <p>The wave equation describes the acceleration of the string so we
  need to specify its initial position and initial velocity.</p>
    </introduction>
    <task workspace="3.5in">
      <statement>
        <p>How does the form of the solution simplify when the string is
  initially at rest: <m>\ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial t}(x,0) = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-5-soln">
      <p>
        <md>
          \pd{u}{t}=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} -A_n \left(\frac{n\pi v}{L}\right)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) +  B_n \left(\frac{n \pi v}{L}\right)\cos\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)
        </md>
        so 
        <md>
          \pd{u}{t}(x,0)=\sum_{n=1}^{\infty} 0 +  B_n \left(\frac{n \pi v}{L}\right)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) = 0
        </md>
        Since <m>n, v, L</m> are non-zero constants and sine is a function that is not zero for every <m>x</m>, then
        <m>{B_n=0}</m> for all <m>n</m>.  Thus the general solution would simplify to
        <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \cos\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) 
         </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
    <task workspace="3in">
      <statement>
        <p>What if we had a different initial condition instead of the initial velocity being 0?  How does the form of the solution simplify when the string is
  initially at equilibrium: <m>u(x,0) = 0</m>.</p>
      </statement>
    <solution component="S2-5-soln">
      <p>
        <md>
          u(x,0) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) +  0 = 0
         </md>
         which means <m>{A_n = 0}</m> for all <m>n</m> and thus the solution would 
         simplify to 
        
          <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty}  B_n \sin\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)
         </md>
         
      </p>
    </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>Suppose that the string is initially at rest and <m>u(x,0) =
  f(x)</m>.  The double angle formulas say that
  <md>
    \ds \sin(\alpha)\cos(\beta) = \frac12\left(\sin(\alpha+\beta) +
      \sin(\alpha-\beta)\right).
  </md>
  Use this fact to show that the wave breaks into two pieces: a left-
  and right-traveling wave.
  <md>
  \ds u(x,t) = \frac12(f(x+vt) + f(x-vt)).
  </md></p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-5-soln">
      <p>
        Since the string is initially at rest, that means <m>\pd u t(x,0) = 0</m> and so we simplify to part 2a,  
        <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \cos\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) 
         </md>
         Then applying the double angle formula with <m>{\alpha = \frac{n\pi x}{L}}</m>
         and <m>{\beta = \frac{n\pi v t}L}</m> we have
         <md>
          u(x,t) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty A_n \frac12 \left[\sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L} + \frac{n \pi v t}{L}\right) + \sin\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L} - \frac{n \pi v t}{L}\right) \right]
        </md>
        <md>
          = \sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{A_n}{2} \left[\sin\left(\frac{n\pi}{L}(x+vt)\right) + \sin\left(\frac{n\pi }{L} (x-vt)\right) \right]
        </md>
        and since <m>u(x,0) = f(x)=\sum_{n=1}^\infty A_n \sin\left(\frac{n\pi}{L}(x)\right)</m>
        then <md>
          u(x,t) = \frac12(f(x+vt) + f(x-vt)).
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <exercise workspace="2.5in">
    <statement>
      <p>How do the solutions change if the ends are ``free'' rather than
  clamped: 
  <md> 
<mrow>\ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(0,t) \amp  =  0</mrow>
<mrow>\ds \frac{\partial u}{\partial x}(L,t) \amp  =  0</mrow>
</md></p>
    </statement>
    <solution component="S2-5-soln">
      <p>
        The Sturm-Liouville problem becomes <m>{X''=-kX, X'(0)=0, X'(L)=0}</m>
        which has solution 
        <m>{k=0, X_0 = 1}</m> and <m>{k=\left(\frac{n\pi}L\right)^2, X_n = \cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}L\right)}</m>. 
        </p>
      <p>
        Then the temporal differential equation also splits into two pieces:
        when <m>{k=0}</m> we are solving <m>T_0 '' = 0</m> which has solution
        <m>{T_0 = A_0t+B_0}</m>.  Then for <m>{n=1,2,...}</m> we are solving 
        <m>{T_n'' = -\left(\frac{n\pi v}L\right)^2 T_n}</m> which has solutions of 
        sine and cosine, <m>\cos\left(\frac{n\pi v t}L\right)</m> or <m>\sin\left(\frac{n\pi v t}L\right)</m>. 
        So the general solution is 
        <md>
          u(x,t) =A_0t + B_0 + \sum_{n=1}^{\infty} A_n \cos\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right) +  B_n \sin\left(\frac{n\pi v t}{L}\right)\cos\left(\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)
        </md>
        
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
